// A `Simd<T>#(N)` must be exactly 128 bits. 128 is the only width EVERY kama target has — SSE2 on the
// x86-64 baseline, NEON on aarch64, wasm128 since the driver started passing -msimd128 — and a wider one
// needs `-march`, a CPU-tuning flag kama does not have yet.
//
// The check lives in registerSimd rather than as a `comptime assert` in the prelude declaration, which is
// what the design first specified. It cannot go there: an intrinsic's prelude declaration is
// documentation-only with an EMPTY body, so an assert written in it would never run.
fn int32 main() {
    Simd<int32>#(3) v = [1, 2, 3];   // 12 bytes, not 16
    return v.lane(index: 0);
}
